digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56897-5
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This research proposes to develop and evaluate a late-stage PET tracer production strategy based on direct radiofluorination of α-bromo-α-fluoroalkane precursors with no-carrier-added [18F]fluoride, aiming to access high-molar-activity 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes while treating automation amenability and broad candidate-tracer application as design and deployment goals.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired conclusions jointly identify the PET-tracer motivation, the value of 18F and CF2H motifs, the methodological gap for alkane α,α-difluoromethyl labeling, the plausible direct-radiofluorination strategy, and the operational/application goals to be evaluated."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) PET molecular imaging depends on well-designed radiotracers because such tracers allow PET to report specific biological targets and in vivo biological processes."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Given the need for 18F-compatible methods and the plausible direct-substitution scenario, direct radiofluorination of α-halo-α-fluoroalkanes with [18F]fluoride is a plausible research response for accessing 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The proposed method is to develop direct radiofluorination of α-bromo-α-fluoroalkane precursors with [18F]fluoride as a late-stage route to high-molar-activity 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) If a new 18F-CF2H labeling method expands PET-tracer chemical space, it should be leveraged by applying it to diverse candidate PET tracers."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The methodology should therefore be applied broadly to install the 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethyl moiety into diverse candidate PET tracers."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because PET development depends on access to appropriate radiotracers and demand is growing for methods that access 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethyl moieties, new methods to access 18F-CF2H motifs are valuable for PET-tracer development."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The cited earlier alkane-CF2H radiosynthetic examples illustrate concrete limitations, including low yield, carrier-added fluoride use, oxidant requirement, and metal-ligand mediation, rather than proving by themselves that all existing methods fail broadly."];
  "nI_18Fbest" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given fluorine-18 is accessible and imaging-friendly and it has been widely used in successful tracers, therefore fluorine-18 is a practical/default radionuclide to focus labeling-method development on."];
  "nI_18Fconstraint_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a labeling method requires harsh conditions, long reaction times, or causes high carrier dilution, then it is incompatible with fluorine-18 radiosynthesis and high molar activity requirements."];
  "nI_CF2Hvalue" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given CF2H can act as a hydrogen-bond donor and CF2H can improve pharmacokinetic/physicochemical properties, therefore CF2H is a high-value motif to support in tracer and drug design."];
  "nI_abd_hypothesis" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given there is a need for a simple high-A_m method and α-fluoroalkanes with α-leaving groups could undergo [18F]fluoride substitution to give gem-difluorides without carrier dilution, therefore a plausible solution is direct radiofluorination of α-halo-α-fluoroalkanes with [18F]fluoride."];
  "nI_abd_hypothesis_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If an α-fluoroalkane also bears a good leaving group on the α-carbon, then nucleophilic [18F]fluoride substitution at that carbon can form a geminal difluoride (RCF2H) while introducing 18F without adding carrier fluoride, enabling high molar activity."];
  "nI_application_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a new 18F-labeling method expands accessible motifs and PET utility depends on radiotracer diversity, then the method should be applied broadly to diverse candidate PET tracers."];
  "nI_compete_dilute_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If carrier fluoride is generated during radiofluorination, then it competes with [18F]fluoride and causes isotopic dilution that lowers molar activity."];
  "nI_gap_alkane" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given arene/heteroarene routes exist but are complex/mediocre and arene-focused methods are unsuitable for efficient alkane CF2H labeling, therefore there is an unmet need for efficient 18F-labeling of α,α-difluoromethylalkanes."];
  "nI_lowAm_mech" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given C–F cleavage can generate carrier fluoride and carrier fluoride competes with [18F]fluoride causing dilution, therefore C–F-bond cleavage tends to reduce molar activity."];
  "nI_methodproposal_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If there is a clear need for high-A_m 18F-labeling of α,α-difluoromethylalkanes and a plausible direct-substitution hypothesis exists, then developing a direct radiofluorination method for α-halo-α-fluoroalkanes is justified."];
  "nI_need_direct18F" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given non-radioactive difluoromethylation methods require harsh/slow conditions and likely high carrier dilution and such requirements are incompatible with 18F radiosynthesis and high A_m, therefore new 18F-compatible (mild/fast/high-A_m) methods are required."];
  "nI_statusquo" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given nucleophilic substitution is the frequent 18F-tracer method and these methods give acceptable yields and high A_m, therefore baseline 18F radiochemistry is mature for single-fluorine incorporation."];
  "nR2_2" [label="(2,1,2) Currently, PET molecular imaging is sensitive to biological processes."];
  "nS10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, the most frequently used 18F-tracer preparation methods are nucleophilic substitutions on good leaving groups (aliphatic/aromatic)."];
  "nS11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, these nucleophilic-substitution methods reliably give acceptable yields and high molar activity (A_m) for many 18F tracers."];
  "nS16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, CF2H can act as a hydrogen-bond donor due to a highly polarized C–H bond, distinguishing it from other polyfluorinated motifs."];
  "nS17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, CF2H can be a metabolically stable bioisostere (of OH/SH/NH2) and can improve pharmacokinetic/physicochemical properties."];
  "nS2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, PET's broad utility derives from well-designed radiotracers that report specific protein targets and in vivo processes."];
  "nS23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, demand is growing for novel methods to access 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethyl moieties to expand PET-tracer chemical space."];
  "nS25" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, methods often give low molar activity because precursors with pre-existing C–F bonds can cleave during labeling, generating carrier fluoride that competes and dilutes A_m."];
  "nS27" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, these arene/heteroarene methods require specialized precursors/metal catalysts/post-manipulations and give mediocre molar activities."];
  "nS29" [label="(29,0,0) Currently, despite being well-established for arenes, these methods are unsuitable for efficient synthesis of 18F-labeled α,α-difluoromethylalkanes."];
  "nS31" [label="(31,0,0) Currently, those non-radioactive difluoromethylation methods are not adaptable to 18F labeling because they require harsh conditions, long times, and likely cause high carrier dilution."];
  "nS33" [label="(33,0,0) Currently, the earliest approach gave only one example in low yield and used oxidant plus large excess carrier-added fluoride reagent."];
  "nS37" [label="(37,0,0) Currently, that single-step method also requires an oxidizing agent and a metal-ligand mediator."];
  "nS39" [label="(39,0,0) Currently, a plausible scenario for a straightforward high-A_m route is treating an α-fluoroalkane that has an α-leaving group with [18F]fluoride."];
  "nS4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, fluorine-18 is accessible in no-carrier-added form from cyclotrons and has favorable properties for imaging and tracer design."];
  "nS9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, fluorine-18 has been prominently featured in many successful PET tracers since the late 1970s."];
  "nS4" -> "nI_18Fbest" [label="induction-case"];
  "nS9" -> "nI_18Fbest" [label="induction-common"];
